{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["And the reason of <b>For this commandment</b> — [it refers] to the entire Torah in its entirety. But the correct interpretation is that concerning the entire Torah it says (Deuteronomy 8:1), \"The whole commandment that I command you this day,\" whereas <b>this commandment</b> refers to the repentance mentioned above: \"And you shall bring back to your heart\" (Deuteronomy 30:1) and \"And you shall return to YHWH your Elohim\" (Deuteronomy 30:2) — a commandment that He commands us to do so. And it is stated in the binyan (intermediate, habitual) conjugation, to hint within the promise that the matter is destined to come to pass. And the reason for saying \"For if your outcast be at the edge of heaven\" and you are in the hand of the nations — you are able to return to YHWH and to do all that I command you this day, is that the matter is not wondrous and distant from you, but very near to you, to do it at all times and in every place. And this is the meaning of \"in your mouth and in your heart to do it\" — that they shall confess their iniquity and the iniquity of their fathers with their mouth, and return in their heart to YHWH, and accept upon themselves this day the Torah to do it for generations, as He mentioned (Deuteronomy 30:2), \"you and your children, with all your heart,\" as I have explained (there)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:57:39.354710917Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}